call_details.response:
The current position has exactly three legal moves: columns 1, 5 and 6. No move produces an immediate win for x, nor does any leave an immediate win for o.

Column 6 is the unique move that creates a level-1 threat (three consecutive x vertically on column 6, with the next square open). Consequently o is forced to answer on column 6; any other reply lets x finish the vertical four on the subsequent turn. After the forced exchange the same forcing sequence continues:

- x drops on column 5, creating a new level-1 threat (horizontal four on row 3);
- o is forced to block;
- the block itself produces a level-1 threat for o (horizontal four on row 2), which x must answer.

The resulting position contains only two remaining winning patterns:

- x can still finish a horizontal four on row 1 by occupying both remaining squares of that line;
- o can still finish a horizontal four on row 0 by occupying both remaining squares of that line.

With optimal play each side can always deny the opponent’s pattern while protecting its own, so the game ends in a draw after the board is filled. The same draw is reachable from the other two opening moves, but only column 6 seizes the initiative and forces o’s replies from the first turn onward. All alternative lines that deviate from the forcing sequence allow the opponent to seize a comparable or stronger initiative; therefore column 6 is optimal.

Final Answer: 6

